  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Hypholoma capnoides, commonly known as the Smoky-gilled Hypholoma, is a small to medium-sized wood-decomposing mushroom with a distinctive smoky gray coloration on its gills. It belongs to the Strophariaceae family and is frequently encountered in New England during fall months.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Smoky Gray Gills",
      "description": "The gills are characteristically gray to smoky-colored, becoming darker with age, which distinguishes it from similar pale-gilled species.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Clustered Growth",
      "description": "Fruiting bodies typically emerge in dense clusters or tufts on decaying wood, logs, and stumps.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap and Stem Color",
      "description": "The cap ranges from yellow-brown to reddish-brown with a paler margin, and the stem is thin and fibrous.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Hypholoma capnoides thrives on decaying hardwood logs, stumps, and woody debris in forests, woodlands, and disturbed areas throughout New England, particularly during autumn.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its edible reputation in some regions, Smoky-gilled Hypholoma can cause gastrointestinal upset in some individuals, making proper identification and caution essential before consumption.",
